Grafana alerting drill — TimeLakeDB
2026-08-21 | Grafana 13.1.3 | deploy/compose/timelakedb-alerting.yml
Drill: deploy/compose/alert-drill/alert_drill.sh
Doc:   docs/ALERTING.md

Two runs are recorded. The green one on its own would not be evidence of
anything: a drill that cannot fail passes for free. The second run is the
same drill against provisioning mutated ASC -> DESC, which is the exact
defect the drill exists to catch.

==============================================================================
RUN 1 — stock provisioning, cold stack. Expected: pass.
==============================================================================
==============================================================================
  Grafana alerting drill against TimeLakeDB
==============================================================================

=== A. the stack is up ===
  PASS  TimeLakeDB answers /health
  PASS  Grafana answers /api/health
  PASS  the notification sink is listening
  PASS  starting from an empty alertprobe table

=== B. Grafana can read TimeLakeDB over Flight SQL ===
  PASS  seeded low values (10) at t-600s..t-300s
  PASS  the provisioned datasource returns rows
  PASS  the frame has a time field and a numeric field (reducible)

=== C. both rules are provisioned and evaluating ===
  PASS  rule 'alertprobe value high' is loaded
  PASS  negative control 'ordering guard (must not fire)' is loaded
  PASS  rule evaluates without error (health=ok)

=== D. below the threshold, nothing fires ===
  PASS  value 10 vs threshold 50 -> Normal

=== E. above the threshold, the correct rule fires ===
  PASS  seeded high values (100) at t-120s..now
  PASS  value 100 vs threshold 50 -> Alerting
  PASS  the mis-ordered guard stayed inactive while the correct rule fired

=== F. the notification was actually delivered ===
  PASS  the sink received 1 notification group(s)
  PASS  the delivered payload names the firing rule

=== G. it resolves when the data comes back down ===
  PASS  seeded low values (5) as the newest rows
  PASS  the alert cleared back to Normal

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  rule states at exit:
    alertprobe value high              inactive   ok
    ordering guard (must not fire)     inactive   ok
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  18 passed, 0 failed
  DRILL PASSED

==============================================================================
RUN 2 — same drill, `ORDER BY time ASC` mutated to DESC in the provisioned
rules and nothing else changed. Expected: fail at phase E.

This is the mutation that matters. The rule still evaluates, still reports
health=ok, and never fires. Phases F and G report SKIP rather than PASS —
delivery and recovery cannot be demonstrated for an alert that never fired,
and scoring them green would turn one real failure into a mostly-green run.
==============================================================================
==============================================================================
  Grafana alerting drill against TimeLakeDB
==============================================================================

=== A. the stack is up ===
  PASS  TimeLakeDB answers /health
  PASS  Grafana answers /api/health
  PASS  the notification sink is listening
  PASS  starting from an empty alertprobe table

=== B. Grafana can read TimeLakeDB over Flight SQL ===
  PASS  seeded low values (10) at t-600s..t-300s
  PASS  the provisioned datasource returns rows
  PASS  the frame has a time field and a numeric field (reducible)

=== C. both rules are provisioned and evaluating ===
  PASS  rule 'alertprobe value high' is loaded
  PASS  negative control 'ordering guard (must not fire)' is loaded
  PASS  rule evaluates without error (health=ok)

=== D. below the threshold, nothing fires ===
  PASS  value 10 vs threshold 50 -> Normal

=== E. above the threshold, the correct rule fires ===
  PASS  seeded high values (100) at t-120s..now
  FAIL  the rule did NOT fire on data above its threshold
        state=inactive health=ok -- note health is 'ok': that is the ORDER BY trap, see docs/ALERTING.md
  SKIP  whether the mis-ordered guard held (it is inactive)
        the correct rule never fired, so nothing distinguishes the guard from it

=== F. the notification was actually delivered ===
  SKIP  notification delivery
        the rule never fired, so Grafana had nothing to send and a silent sink proves nothing

=== G. it resolves when the data comes back down ===
  SKIP  recovery to Normal
        the rule never left Normal, so returning to it demonstrates nothing

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  rule states at exit:
    alertprobe value high              inactive   ok
    ordering guard (must not fire)     inactive   ok
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  12 passed, 1 failed, 3 skipped
  DRILL FAILED
